diff --git a/changelog.d/3692-ota-rollout-audit.security.md b/changelog.d/3692-ota-rollout-audit.security.md
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..904814301
--- /dev/null
+++ b/changelog.d/3692-ota-rollout-audit.security.md
@@ -0,0 +1,11 @@
+- **Changing an OTA update's rollout percentage is now recorded in the audit log
+  (#3692).** The route that decides which endpoints receive a given agent binary
+  gated correctly on admin, then wrote only to the application log — so "who
+  de-prioritised this security patch, and when" was not answerable from the audit
+  trail an incident responder or SOC 2 auditor reads. The new
+  `ota.package.rollout_changed` event records the acting principal, the package
+  key, and crucially the value the rollout changed **from**: a package sitting at
+  0% looks identical whether it was deliberately pulled back or never rolled out,
+  and only the prior value separates them. Requests naming a package that does
+  not exist record `not_found` rather than a fictional success, so key
+  enumeration stays visible rather than blending into normal traffic.
diff --git a/docs/user-manual/audit-log.md b/docs/user-manual/audit-log.md
index ba0fb7ecd..4550c8f6c 100644
--- a/docs/user-manual/audit-log.md
+++ b/docs/user-manual/audit-log.md
@@ -268,6 +268,7 @@ required.
 | `execution.read` | Execution | A management-group-confined caller was refused a legacy `/api/executions/{id}` read — `GET .../{id}` (`surface=detail`), `.../summary` (`surface=summary`), `.../agents` (`surface=agents`), or `.../children` (`surface=children`) (#3789). Emitted **only when the requesting caller's fleet-read scope was engaged** (`require_fleet_read`'s `gate.scope`) — an UNCONFINED caller's plain "this id has never existed" 404 is not a confinement decision and writes no row, so this verb's rate is a measure of confinement actually firing, not of ordinary 404 traffic (a caller-global-grant/admin holder therefore never appears here for a bad id). `GET /api/executions` (list) never emits this verb — its confinement is a silent SQL `WHERE`-clause narrowing with no per-row audit, same as every other pushdown-filtered list read. `target_id` is the execution id; `detail="not found or outside caller's fleet-read scope surface=<detail\|summary\|agents\|children> cid=<correlation_id>"` — the detail is deliberately IDENTICAL whether the execution never existed or exists but is fully out of scope (a distinguishing detail string would be a same-response-shape oracle a confined caller could probe). `result=denied`. Permission gate: `require_fleet_read("Execution","Read")` (#3789 — replaced the flat `Execution:Read` gate on these four routes). |
 | `execution.rerun` | execution (lowercase — matches this verb's pre-existing `success`/`failure` rows, NOT the `Execution`-capitalized convention `execution.read` and most other verbs in this table use; a `target_type=Execution` SIEM filter will not match any row this verb ever writes) | `POST /api/executions/{id}/rerun`. `result=success`: `target_id` is the NEW rerun execution's id (not the original). `result=denied` (#3789, confined callers only — an unconfined caller's failed rerun falls through unaudited to `create_rerun`'s own pre-existing 400, unchanged from pre-#3789 behavior): the original execution failed the mutation-admission rule in `execution_scope_rules.hpp` (`admit_confined_mutation` — nonexistent, zero-visible, an incomplete target-response ledger, or any out-of-scope targeted agent all collapse to this SAME outcome, by design, to avoid a hidden-cohort oracle); `target_id` is the ORIGINAL execution id; `detail="not found or outside caller's fleet-read scope cid=<correlation_id>"`. Permission gate: `require_permission("Execution","Execute")` ahead of `require_fleet_read("Execution","Read")` for scope (the fleet gate is structurally Read-only, `authz_gates.cpp`, and cannot replace the Execute check). |
 | `execution.cancel` | execution (lowercase — same casing anomaly as `execution.rerun` above) | `POST /api/executions/{id}/cancel`. `result=success`/`result=failure` cover the pre-#3789 `mark_cancelled` outcome, unchanged. `result=denied` (#3789, confined callers only — see `execution.rerun` above for the identical admission rule and no-oracle rationale) uses the same `target_id`/`detail` shape as `execution.rerun`'s denied row. An UNCONFINED caller hitting a genuinely nonexistent id also gets a plain `404` with **no** audit row — the response CODE differs from `execution.rerun`'s unconfined-not-found `400` (see above), but both write no audit row in this case. This closes what `mark_cancelled` alone would otherwise report as a false `success` on a zero-row UPDATE. Permission gate: same as `execution.rerun`. |
+| `ota.package.rollout_changed` | UpdatePackage | An admin changed the rollout percentage of an agent OTA update package via Settings → OTA Updates. `target_id` is `<platform>/<arch>/<version>`; `detail` carries `from=<N>% to=<M>% mandatory=true\|false`. **The `from=` value is the point of this row.** A rollout of 0% cannot, on its own, distinguish a mandatory security patch that was deliberately pulled back from one that was never rolled out — `from=100% to=0% mandatory=true` can, and that is the scenario this audit exists to make reconstructable. The percentage is clamped to 0–100 server-side and the row reports the **clamped, stored** value, not the requested one. `result=success` when a matching package was found and updated. A request naming a package that does not exist changes nothing and records `result=denied` with `detail=not_found` — the same shape this file's other rejection branches use, so the `result == "denied"` filter described under **Result vocabulary** surfaces it along with every other probe. |
 | `plugin_signing.bundle.uploaded` | PluginTrustBundle | An admin uploaded a PEM trust bundle via Settings → Plugin Code Signing. `detail` carries `<N> cert(s), sha256=<hex>` on success. On `result=failure` (PEM validation rejected the input — empty body, missing markers, no parsable cert, hashing failure, etc.) `detail` carries the validation error string. Audit row is emitted on both branches so a SIEM rule on `failure` can detect malformed-upload probing. |
 | `plugin_signing.bundle.cleared` | PluginTrustBundle | An admin removed the trust bundle (Settings → Plugin Code Signing → Remove). `detail` carries `file removed` (a file existed and was deleted) or `no file present` (clear was a no-op). Always emits `success` unless the require-flag DB write fails first, in which case `result=failure` and `detail` carries the store error — and the file is **not** removed (two-phase commit prevents the disk/DB-flag desync). |
 | `plugin_signing.require.changed` | RuntimeConfig | An admin toggled "Require signed plugins" via Settings. `target_id` is `plugin_signing_required`, `detail` carries the new value (`"true"` or `"false"`). Emitted on every change including no-op idempotent re-saves. |
